# Line endings.
#
# Everything in this repository that runs, runs on Linux. deploy.sh is
# extracted from a `git archive` tarball straight onto the server and
# executed; docker-entrypoint.sh is the container's ENTRYPOINT; the two
# scripts/*.sh are spawned by both deploy.sh and the test suite.
#
# A CRLF line ending on any of them is not cosmetic. The kernel reads the
# shebang literally, so `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r` sends it looking for an
# interpreter named "bash\r" and the script dies before its first line:
#
#     env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory
#
# That is exactly what happened on the first deploy attempted from a Windows
# checkout. The repository stores LF, but core.autocrlf hands CRLF to the
# working tree -- and `git archive` inherits that conversion, so the tarball
# uploaded to the box carried CRLF scripts even though the committed blobs
# were clean. The container's ENTRYPOINT would have failed the same way, so
# this was one step away from a stack that builds and never starts.
#
# eol=lf makes the working tree, the repository and every archive agree, on
# every platform, which is the only arrangement in which "what runs on the
# box is what the tests ran" is actually true.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, never converted or diffed as text.
*.ttf binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.gz binary
*.tar binary
